WEBVTT

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.840
Expertise should be evaluated with evidence, not status alone.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.340
Job titles, credentials, seniority, and confident explanations can all be useful signals,

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.855
but they do not always prove reliable performance.

00:00:10.855 --> 00:00:14.940
Better evidence includes outcomes, consistency, calibration, prediction quality,

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.555
decision records, benchmarks, simulations, and scenario exercises.

00:00:18.555 --> 00:00:20.900
The question is not simply who sounds persuasive.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.125
The question is who performs well under relevant conditions.

00:00:24.125 --> 00:00:25.315
Calibration is especially important.

00:00:25.315 --> 00:00:29.385
A calibrated person has confidence that matches actual likelihood, accuracy,

00:00:29.385 --> 00:00:30.360
or performance.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.640
Poor calibration can appear in two directions.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.100
Someone may be underconfident and fail to act when they have useful knowledge.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.665
Someone else may be overconfident and act with certainty that the evidence does not

00:00:39.665 --> 00:00:40.376
support.

00:00:40.376 --> 00:00:43.180
Expertise includes knowing the difference between what is known, what is likely,

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.085
and what remains uncertain.

00:00:45.085 --> 00:00:48.760
Decision quality is not measured only by whether a single outcome went well.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:52.490
Sometimes a good decision has a bad outcome because conditions were unfavorable.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.758
Sometimes a weak decision succeeds because of luck.

00:00:54.758 --> 00:00:57.880
Decision records help teams examine the reasoning that existed at the time:

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.110
what was known, what assumptions were made, what alternatives were considered,

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.920
and what evidence would have changed the decision.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.390
Simulations and scenario exercises can reveal expertise because they place people in

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.916
realistic constraints without waiting for real consequences.

00:01:09.916 --> 00:01:14.680
They can test pattern recognition, prioritization, communication, and adaptation.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.135
Benchmarks can also help when they reflect the work that matters.

00:01:17.135 --> 00:01:20.510
The best measurements connect to actual performance, not trivia, status,

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.040
or the ability to explain a process without applying it.
